WN.lb(36,{"mt_bZqZQl1tq9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A current of 2 A flows for {a} minutes. How much charge passes, in coulombs?","answer":"120*a","params":{"a":[1,10]},"hint":"{a} minutes is {a} × 60 seconds.","explain":"t = {=60*a} s, so Q = 2 × {=60*a} = {=120*a} C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How many seconds does it take for {a}00 C to pass at a current of 4 A?","answer":"25*a","params":{"a":[1,8]},"hint":"Rearrange Q = I t to get t = Q ÷ I, then divide.","explain":"t = {a}00 ÷ 4 = {=25*a} s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does a current of 1 ampere mean?","choices":["1 coulomb of charge passes a point each second","1 coulomb of charge is used up each second","Each coulomb is given 1 joule of energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Current is a rate of flow.","explain":"1 A = 1 C per second. Energy per coulomb is potential difference, measured in volts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"On a charge–time graph, line X is steeper than line Y. Which has the bigger current?","choices":["X","Y","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The slope is charge ÷ time.","explain":"Gradient = Q ÷ t = I, so the steeper line X has the bigger current."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A torch draws 400 mA for 30 minutes. How much charge passes?","choices":["720 C","12,000 C","12 C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Convert both: mA to A, minutes to seconds.","explain":"0.4 A × 1,800 s = 720 C. 12 C forgets the minutes; 12,000 C forgets the milliamps."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The current through a motor is halved. How long does the same charge now take to pass?","choices":["Twice as long","Half as long","The same time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep Q fixed and use t = Q ÷ I: what happens when I gets smaller?","explain":"With Q fixed, t = Q ÷ I, so halving I doubles t."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia says a lamp in a series loop 'uses up some of the current'. What is wrong with this?","choices":["Charge is not used up; the current is the same all round","Nothing, the current drops after a lamp","The lamp makes extra current"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the counting ring read at every point?","explain":"The same charge passes every point each second, so the current is the same all round. The lamp transfers energy from the charge to its surroundings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which equation gives the current from the charge and the time?","choices":["I = Q ÷ t","I = Q × t","I = t ÷ Q"],"answer":0,"hint":"Amps are coulombs per second.","explain":"Rearranging Q = I t gives I = Q ÷ t, which matches the unit C/s."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A current of {a} A flows through a lamp for 5 minutes. How much charge passes, in coulombs?","answer":"300*a","params":{"a":[1,6]},"hint":"Change 5 minutes to seconds first, then use Q = I × t.","explain":"5 minutes = 300 s. Q = {a} A × 300 s = {=300*a} C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A charge of {a}0 C flows past a point in 5 s. What is the current, in amps?","answer":"2*a","params":{"a":[1,9]},"hint":"Current is charge per second: I = Q ÷ t.","explain":"I = {a}0 C ÷ 5 s = {=2*a} A."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A current of 250 mA flows for 40 s. How much charge passes?","choices":["10 C","10,000 C","0.16 C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change milliamps to amps first: divide by 1,000.","explain":"250 mA = 0.25 A. Q = 0.25 × 40 = 10 C. Using 250 gives 10,000 C, a thousand times too big."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In a series circuit the ammeter before the lamp reads 0.6 A. What does an ammeter after the lamp read?","choices":["0.6 A","Less than 0.6 A","0 A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is charge used up as it passes through the lamp?","explain":"Charge is not used up, so the current is the same at every point in a series loop: 0.6 A."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How long does it take for 90 C to pass at a current of 3 A?","choices":["30 s","270 s","0.03 s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rearrange Q = I t to find t.","explain":"t = Q ÷ I = 90 ÷ 3 = 30 s. Multiplying gives 270, which has the wrong units."}],"explain":{"kid":["Current is how much charge flows past a point each second. We count charge in coulombs, C. We measure current in amps, A. One amp means one coulomb goes past each second.","So charge equals current times time: Q = I × t. Two amps for three seconds lets six coulombs pass. To find the time, flip it round: t = Q ÷ I. Half the current takes twice as long.","Time must be in seconds. Five minutes is 300 seconds, not 5. Current must be in amps. 200 mA is 0.2 A. Change the units first, then use the rule.","In one loop, the current is the same all the way round. A lamp does not use up charge. It takes energy from the charge as it flows through."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. AQA GCSE Physics (8463, 4.2.1.3) defines current as the rate of flow of electrical charge and requires Q = I t, with charge in coulombs, current in amperes and time in seconds; it also states that the current is the same at any point in a single closed loop. OCR Gateway and Edexcel specifications set the same equation. Key words: charge, coulomb, current, ampere, rate of flow, series circuit, milliamp.","In SI terms one coulomb is the charge carried by a current of 1 A in 1 s. The elementary charge is exactly 1.602176634 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, so 1 C is about 6.24 × 10¹⁸ electrons. On a charge–time graph a steady current gives a straight line through the origin whose gradient is the current.","The most common mistake is leaving time in minutes or hours, or current in milliamps, before substituting. Another is inverting the rearrangement, for example giving I = t ÷ Q. A persistent misconception is that current is used up by a lamp, so it is smaller after the lamp than before it.","In the lab your child sets a current so that 6 C passes a gate in 3 s, finds the currents that move 12 C in 4 s and in 8 s, answers three unit-trap questions, drags charge–time lines to slopes of 2 A and 3.5 A, and reads the current at four points round a series loop."]},"familyTip":"Find the rating on a phone charger or a power bank, often written in mA or mAh. Ask: if the charger gives 2 A for one hour, how many coulombs flow? (2 × 3,600 = 7,200 C.) Then ask how long a 500 mA current would take to move the same charge.","keyIdea":"Electric current is the rate of flow of charge: the number of coulombs that pass a point each second. So charge = current × time, Q = I t, with charge Q in coulombs (C), current I in amperes (A) and time t in seconds (s). A current of 1 A means 1 C passes each second. Rearranged, I = Q ÷ t and t = Q ÷ I. Convert minutes and hours to seconds and milliamps to amps before you use the equation. In a single series loop the current is the same at every point, because charge is not used up; a lamp transfers energy, not charge.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which of these let exactly 6 coulombs of charge pass? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["6 C passes","A different amount"],"items":[["2 A for 3 s",0],["3 A for 2 s",0],["0.5 A for 12 s",0],["6 A for 1 s",0],["2 A for 4 s",1],["3 A for 3 s",1],["6 mA for 1 s",1],["1 A for 1 minute",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagqit","caption":"Flagship lab: count charge through a gate, find the time for a fixed charge, beat the unit traps, draw charge–time lines, check the current round a loop.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_TGEzxumdYt":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A flat pd–time graph means…","choices":["d.c.","a.c.","zero frequency only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flat means the pd does not reverse.","explain":"A steady one-way pd is direct current."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A pd–time wave through zero means…","choices":["a.c.","d.c. from a cell","a broken meter only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Crossing zero shows a reverse.","explain":"When pd swings both ways the supply is alternating current."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"At {a} Hz, cycles in 10 seconds?","answer":"10*a","hint":"Multiply frequency by ten seconds.","explain":"{a} × 10 = {=10*a} cycles in 10 seconds.","params":{"a":[45,55]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"US mains is about…","choices":["120 V at 60 Hz","230 V at 50 Hz","12 V d.c."],"answer":0,"hint":"US values differ from UK mains.","explain":"Typical US mains is about 120 V at 60 Hz."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Best first step to tell a.c. from d.c. is…","choices":["look at the pd–time graph shape","guess from the wire colour","ignore the time axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the graph shape carefully.","explain":"Graph shape shows whether pd stays flat or reverses."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{a} cycles happen in 1 second. Frequency in Hz?","answer":"a","hint":"Hertz means cycles per second.","explain":"{a} cycles in one second means frequency {=a} Hz.","params":{"a":[40,70]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The key skill here vs current-and-voltage is…","choices":["telling a.c. from d.c. with graphs and mains values","only defining I = V/R in a series loop","drawing series and parallel symbols only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Focus on a.c. versus d.c. here.","explain":"Here you compare a.c. and d.c. Circuit equations live in the current-and-voltage lesson."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a.c. the wires…","choices":["keep their metal atoms; pd flips","physically swap ends each cycle","become d.c. after one second"],"answer":0,"hint":"What moves electrically in the wire?","explain":"Atoms stay; the potential difference reverses direction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A car battery is typically…","choices":["about 12 V d.c.","230 V a.c.","50 Hz only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Vehicles use cells, not mains.","explain":"Car batteries supply roughly 12 V direct current."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A battery supplies…","choices":["direct current (d.c.)","alternating current (a.c.)","no potential difference"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cells keep charge flowing one way.","explain":"Batteries and cells supply direct current. Mains supplies alternating current."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A supply runs at {a} Hz. How many cycles in 2 seconds?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Frequency counts cycles each second; multiply by 2.","explain":"At {a} Hz there are {a} cycles per second, so 2 seconds give {=2*a} cycles.","params":{"a":[40,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"UK mains is about…","choices":["230 V at 50 Hz","50 V at 230 Hz","12 V d.c."],"answer":0,"hint":"Voltage is hundreds of volts; frequency is tens of hertz.","explain":"UK mains is about 230 V at 50 Hz. Do not swap the numbers."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Frequency {a} Hz for {b} seconds. Total cycles?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply frequency by time in seconds.","explain":"{a} cycles each second for {b} seconds is {=a*b} cycles.","params":{"a":[40,60],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["a battery supplies a.c.","a battery supplies d.c.","UK mains is about 50 Hz"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spot the false claim about batteries.","explain":"Batteries supply d.c., not a.c. Mains frequency about 50 Hz is true for the UK."}],"explain":{"kid":["Direct current (d.c.) from a cell or battery pushes charge one way. On a potential-difference against time graph it looks like a flat line.","Alternating current (a.c.) from the mains reverses direction many times each second. Its pd–time graph is a wave that crosses zero.","In the UK, mains is about 230 volts at 50 hertz. Frequency in hertz is how many full cycles happen each second. In the US it is about 120 V at 60 Hz.","In a.c., the potential difference (and the current) reverse each half-cycle. The metal atoms in the wire stay put."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. KS4 Electricity: distinguish a.c. and d.c., sketch pd–time graphs, and state local mains voltage and frequency. Separate from the circuit-quantity lesson on current and voltage.","Words: alternating current, direct current, potential difference, frequency, hertz, mains, half-cycle. Common mistakes: calling battery supply a.c.; swapping 230 V with 50 Hz; thinking frequency is a voltage; saying nothing reverses in a.c.","In the lab the learner reveals both graphs, recalls UK mains values, names what reverses, spots the battery-as-a.c. myth, and orders the check."]},"familyTip":"Ask: is a phone battery a.c. or d.c.? Then: what do the 230 V and 50 Hz on a UK appliance label mean?","keyIdea":"Direct current (d.c.) from cells and batteries keeps one direction; alternating current (a.c.) from the mains reverses each half-cycle. UK mains is about 230 V at 50 Hz. Read pd–time graphs: flat for d.c., waving through zero for a.c.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"a.c./d.c. idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["battery → d.c.",0],["mains → a.c.",0],["battery → a.c.",1],["UK mains ≈ 230 V, 50 Hz",0],["a.c. never reverses",1],["pd–time wave means a.c.",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagacdc","caption":"Flagship lab: show d.c. and a.c. pd–time graphs, reveal UK 230 V / 50 Hz, name what reverses, catch mix-ups, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_prqHs1rg3D":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the potential of the neutral wire in normal use?","choices":["At or close to 0 V","About 230 V","About 115 V"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which wire carries the 230 V from the supply?","explain":"The neutral wire completes the circuit and is at or close to 0 V. The live wire is at about 230 V."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"In a UK plug, where does the brown wire go?","choices":["Bottom right, through the fuse","Bottom left","The top terminal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember BRown goes Bottom Right, through the fuse.","explain":"The brown live wire goes to the bottom right terminal through the fuse. Blue goes bottom left and green-and-yellow earth goes to the top."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A hair dryer is rated 1200 W on 230 V. Which fuse should it have: 3 A, 5 A or 13 A?","choices":["13 A","5 A","3 A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out I = P ÷ V first. The fuse must be above it.","explain":"I = 1200 ÷ 230 ≈ 5.2 A, which is more than 5 A. A 5 A fuse would melt in normal use, so it needs the next size up, 13 A."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A kettle is rated {=230*k} W on 230 V. What is its normal current, in amps?","answer":"k","hint":"I = P ÷ V, with V = 230 V.","explain":"I = {=230*k} ÷ 230 = {k} A. That is why kettles have a 13 A fuse.","params":{"k":[9,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why is touching the live wire dangerous?","choices":["There is a large potential difference between it and your body at 0 V","Your body is at 230 V too","The live wire is always very hot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Current flows when there is a potential difference.","explain":"You are at about 0 V and the live wire is at about 230 V, so a current flows through you to earth."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why should a 60 W lamp have a 3 A fuse rather than a 13 A one?","choices":["A 13 A fuse might not melt in a fault, so the thin cable could overheat","A 13 A fuse makes the lamp brighter","A 3 A fuse uses less energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"The lamp takes only about 0.26 A. What does the fuse do?","explain":"The fuse should melt at a current just above normal. A 13 A fuse lets a fault current far bigger than the lamp needs flow, so the cable could overheat before it melts."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A fault makes a current of {i} A flow through the live and earth wires. The fuse is rated at 13 A. By how many amps does the fault current go over the rating?","answer":"i-13","hint":"Take the fuse rating away from the fault current.","explain":"{i} − 13 = {=i-13} A over the rating, so the fuse melts and cuts off the live wire.","params":{"i":[40,100]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A hair straightener has a plastic case and a two-core cable. Why does it need no earth wire?","choices":["It is double insulated, so the case cannot become live","It does not use mains electricity","The fuse does the job of the earth wire"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the case made of? Can it conduct?","explain":"Plastic is an insulator, so a loose live wire cannot make the case live. Double-insulated appliances use two-core cable."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"When does the earth wire carry a current?","choices":["Only if there is a fault","All the time the appliance is on","Only when the appliance is switched off"],"answer":0,"hint":"In normal use, current goes in by the live and back by the neutral.","explain":"The earth wire is a safety wire at 0 V. It only carries a current if a fault connects the live wire to the case."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which wire is brown, carries about 230 V and has the fuse in it?","choices":["Live","Neutral","Earth"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fuse must be able to cut off the 230 V.","explain":"The brown live wire carries the alternating potential difference of about 230 V. The fuse and switch go in it so they can disconnect it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An appliance has a power of {=230*k} W and runs on 230 V mains. What is the normal current, in amps?","answer":"k","hint":"Use I = P ÷ V, with V = 230 V for UK mains.","explain":"I = P ÷ V = {=230*k} ÷ 230 = {k} A. The fuse rating should be just above this.","params":{"k":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A lamp is switched off at its own switch. Is the live wire in its cable safe to touch?","choices":["No: it is still at about 230 V","Yes: switching off makes it 0 V","Yes: only the neutral wire can give a shock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the switch? Is the cable before it still joined to the supply?","explain":"The switch only breaks the circuit at the lamp. The live wire back to the socket is still at 230 V, and your body at 0 V would complete a path to earth."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A heater draws a current of {i} A from the 230 V mains. What is its power in watts?","answer":"230*i","hint":"Rearrange I = P ÷ V into P = I × V.","explain":"P = I × V = {i} × 230 = {=230*i} W.","params":{"i":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A loose live wire touches the metal case of an earthed kettle. What happens?","choices":["A large current flows to earth and the fuse melts","The earth wire stops any current flowing","Nothing, until someone touches the case"],"answer":0,"hint":"The earth wire has a very low resistance.","explain":"The earth wire gives an easy path, so a very large current flows through the live and earth wires. The fuse melts and cuts off the live wire, so the case cannot stay live."}],"explain":{"kid":["Mains electricity in the UK is an alternating current (ac) supply at about 230 V and 50 Hz. Most appliances join to it with a three-core cable. The brown live wire carries the alternating potential difference from the supply. The blue neutral wire completes the circuit and is at or close to 0 V. The green-and-yellow earth wire is at 0 V and only carries a current if something goes wrong.","In a UK plug, the brown live wire goes to the bottom right terminal, through the fuse. The blue neutral wire goes to the bottom left. The earth wire goes to the top terminal, joined to the longest pin. Switches and fuses are always in the live wire, so they can cut off the 230 V.","Your body is at about 0 V, the same as the earth. If you touch the live wire, there is a potential difference of 230 V across you, so a current flows through you to the ground. That is an electric shock, and it can kill. The live wire is still dangerous when an appliance is switched off, because the cable up to the switch is still joined to the supply.","Now suppose a loose live wire touches the metal case of a kettle. Without an earth wire, the case is at 230 V and touching it gives you a shock. With the earth wire joined to the case, the current has a very low-resistance path to earth. A large current flows, the fuse melts and the live wire is cut off. The fuse rating should be just above the normal current, I = P ÷ V. A 60 W lamp needs a 3 A fuse and a 2760 W kettle needs 13 A."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16 (AQA GCSE Physics 4.2.3 and the matching OCR and Edexcel content). Students should name the three wires and their colours, state the potential of each, explain why the live wire is dangerous even when a switch is open, and explain how the earth wire and fuse work together to protect someone when a fault makes a metal case live.","Common mistakes: thinking the earth wire carries current all the time, or that it stops current; thinking the neutral wire is dangerous and the live is safe when off; putting the fuse in the neutral; and believing the biggest fuse is the safest. Students also slip on fuse choice: a 1200 W hair dryer takes about 5.2 A, so a 5 A fuse is too small and it needs 13 A.","The physics behind the danger is potential difference. The body is near earth potential, so contact with the live wire puts about 230 V across the body. The earth wire works because its resistance is very low. A fault then makes a large current, which melts the fuse (or trips a circuit breaker). Appliances with plastic cases are double insulated and use two-core cable with no earth wire.","In the lab students drag each wire to its terminal and get named feedback on wrong ones. They probe the wires with the lamp off and find the live wire in the cable still reads 230 V. They touch an unearthed faulty case, then reconnect the earth and watch the fault current melt the fuse. Last, they work out I = P ÷ V for a lamp, an iron and a kettle and fit 3 A, 5 A and 13 A fuses."]},"familyTip":"Look at the label on a plug or a kettle base together. Find the power in watts, divide it by 230 to get the normal current, and check that the fuse rating (often printed on the fuse or the plug) is just above it. Never open a plug that is plugged in, and never touch the pins.","keyIdea":"UK mains electricity is an alternating supply at about 230 V and 50 Hz. A three-core cable has a brown live wire, which carries the alternating potential difference from the supply; a blue neutral wire, which completes the circuit and is at or close to 0 V; and a green-and-yellow earth wire, which is at 0 V and only carries a current if there is a fault. The fuse and the switch are in the live wire. Your body is at 0 V, so touching the live wire drives a current through you, even when an appliance's switch is off. If a fault makes a metal case live, the earth wire gives a low-resistance path: a large current flows, the fuse melts and the live wire is cut off.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which wire is it: live, neutral or earth? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["🟫 Live","🟦 Neutral","🟩 Earth"],"items":[["Brown, about 230 V",0],["Has the fuse and the switch in it",0],["Blue, completes the circuit",1],["At or close to 0 V in normal use",1],["Green and yellow stripes",2],["Joined to a metal case, carries current only in a fault",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplug","caption":"Flagship lab: wire a UK plug, probe each wire with the switch off, touch a faulty unearthed case, then let the earth wire blow the fuse and pick fuse ratings.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_DEbG-enqpW":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why can rubbing move electrons but not protons?","choices":["Protons are held in the nucleus; outer electrons can be pulled off","Protons are too heavy to see","Electrons are positive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where each particle sits in an atom.","explain":"Protons are bound in the nucleus. Electrons on the outside of surface atoms can be transferred by rubbing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A rod becomes positive. Which statement is right?","choices":["It lost electrons","It gained protons","It gained electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Positive means fewer electrons than protons.","explain":"The rod has fewer electrons than protons because it lost electrons. No protons moved."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A cloth ends up with a charge of −{n} electron charges after rubbing a perspex rod. What is the size of the charge on the rod?","answer":"n","hint":"The rod lost what the cloth gained: equal size, opposite sign.","explain":"The rod lost the {n} electrons the cloth gained, so it is +{n}: the same size, opposite sign.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A negative polythene rod is brought near a positive acetate rod. They ...","choices":["attract","repel","do nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"One rod is negative and one is positive: opposite charges.","explain":"Negative and positive are opposite charges, so they attract."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which sentence about charging by rubbing is true?","choices":["Charge is moved, not made","Charge is made by the rubbing","Charge is destroyed by the rubbing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the two charges after rubbing.","explain":"The two charges are equal and opposite and add to zero, so charge is only moved."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A balloon has {p} protons and gains {g} electrons from hair. It started neutral. How many electrons does it have now?","answer":"p+g","hint":"Neutral means as many electrons as protons. Then add the ones it gained.","explain":"It started with {p} electrons and gained {g}, so {=p+g}. Its charge is −{g}.","params":{"p":[6,12],"g":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which way do electrons move when a polythene rod is rubbed with wool and becomes negative?","choices":["From the wool to the rod","From the rod to the wool","Both ways equally"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one ends up with extra electrons?","explain":"The rod is negative, so it gained electrons from the wool."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A student says: 'The hair gave the balloon its negative charge, so the hair is now neutral.' What is wrong?","choices":["The hair lost electrons, so it is now positive","Nothing is wrong","The hair is now negative too"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does losing electrons do to an object's charge?","explain":"Losing electrons leaves the hair with more protons than electrons, so it is positive by the same amount."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Two objects are rubbed. One ends up −{n}. How many electrons were transferred?","answer":"n","hint":"Each extra electron gives −1.","explain":"A charge of −{n} means {n} extra electrons, so {n} were transferred.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Why does a charged balloon pull charged hair towards it?","choices":["They have opposite charges","They have the same charge","The balloon is magnetic"],"answer":0,"hint":"The balloon is negative. What is the hair?","explain":"The balloon is negative and the hair is positive. Opposite charges attract."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A balloon rubbed on hair becomes negatively charged. What happened?","choices":["Electrons moved from the hair to the balloon","Protons moved from the balloon to the hair","The balloon made new electrons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only one kind of particle can be rubbed off a surface.","explain":"Electrons transfer from the hair to the balloon, so the balloon has extra electrons and is negative. Protons stay in the nuclei."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A polythene rod gains {n} electrons from a wool cloth. What is the charge on the cloth, counted in electron charges? (Write a positive number for a positive charge.)","answer":"n","hint":"The cloth lost exactly the electrons the rod gained.","explain":"The cloth lost {n} electrons, so it has {n} more protons than electrons: +{n}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"An acetate rod is positive after rubbing with a cloth. What charge does the cloth have?","choices":["Negative, the same size","Positive, the same size","No charge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where did the acetate's electrons go?","explain":"The cloth gained the electrons the rod lost, so it is negative by the same amount."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Before rubbing, a rod and a cloth each have charge 0. After rubbing, the rod is −{n}. What is the total charge of the rod and cloth together?","answer":"0*n","hint":"Rubbing moves charge. It does not make or destroy it.","explain":"The cloth is +{n}, and −{n} + {n} = 0. Charge is conserved.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Two balloons are both rubbed on the same hair and hung side by side. What happens?","choices":["They push apart","They pull together","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both gained electrons. What sign does each have?","explain":"Both are negative. Like charges repel, so they push apart."}],"explain":{"kid":["Everything is made of atoms. An atom has positive protons in its middle, called the nucleus. Negative electrons sit round the outside. Most things have the same number of each, so they have no charge.","Rub two things together and some electrons get pulled from one onto the other. The protons stay put. Only electrons move.","The thing that gains electrons now has more negative than positive. It is negative. The thing that loses them is positive by the same amount. If a balloon gains 3 electrons, the hair is +3.","No charge is made. It just moves, so the two charges add up to zero. Then the charged things push or pull. Two negative balloons push apart. A negative balloon and positive hair pull together."],"adult":["Ages 12 to 14. The KS3 physics programme of study in England covers 'separation of positive or negative charges when objects are rubbed together: transfer of electrons, forces between charged objects'. AQA GCSE Physics 4.2.5.1 then asks students to explain how insulators are charged by friction through electron transfer, and NGSS MS-PS2-3 and MS-PS2-5 cover electric forces and fields.","The physics: rubbing brings two surfaces into close contact and electrons transfer to the material that holds them more strongly (the triboelectric effect). Polythene rubbed with wool gains electrons and becomes negative; acetate or perspex rubbed with a cloth loses electrons and becomes positive. The charges are equal and opposite, so charge is conserved. Charge is measured in coulombs; each electron carries 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, and the lab counts whole electrons.","Common mistakes: saying positive charge or protons moved onto an object (a positive object has lost electrons), thinking rubbing creates charge, giving both objects the same sign, thinking the partner stays neutral, and expecting two like-charged balloons to attract. Students also mix up 'gains electrons' with 'becomes positive', because gaining sounds like adding something good.","In the lab your child rubs a balloon on hair and watches 3 electrons hop across while both charges and the total are shown, drags charges between a rod and a cloth (a proton refuses to move), gives the partner's charge in three cases, works out the direction of electron flow from four material clues, and predicts attract or repel for four pairs."]},"familyTip":"Rub a balloon on a wool jumper or dry hair and hold it near the hair: it pulls the strands up. Ask: which one gained electrons? (The balloon, so it is negative.) Then ask what the hair has now: the same amount of positive charge. Rub two balloons and hang them side by side; they push apart because both are negative. Watch for 'the hair gave the balloon positive charge'.","keyIdea":"When two insulators are rubbed together, electrons are transferred from one to the other. The object that gains electrons becomes negatively charged; the object that loses them becomes positively charged by exactly the same amount. Positive charge does not move: protons stay in the nuclei of the atoms. No charge is made or destroyed, so the total charge stays zero. Charged objects then exert forces on each other: like charges repel and opposite charges attract.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"After rubbing, did each object gain or lose electrons? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["gained electrons (negative)","lost electrons (positive)"],"items":[["balloon rubbed on hair",0],["the hair",1],["polythene rod rubbed with wool",0],["the wool",1],["acetate rod rubbed with a cloth",1],["the cloth on the acetate",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrubq","caption":"Flagship lab: rub a balloon and count electrons, move charges by hand, give the equal and opposite charge, and predict forces.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_eTv7W6osCa":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Conventional current leaves a cell from which terminal?","choices":["the positive terminal (long line)","the negative terminal (short line)","both terminals"],"answer":0,"hint":"Conventional means the agreed direction: + to −.","explain":"It is drawn leaving + and returning to −. Electrons do the opposite."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do electrons drift towards the positive terminal?","choices":["They are negative, and opposite charges attract.","They are positive.","The current arrow pushes them."],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the charge on an electron?","explain":"Electrons are negative, so the + terminal attracts them and the − terminal repels them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You redraw a circuit using electron flow instead of conventional current. What happens to the ammeter reading?","choices":["It stays the same.","It becomes negative.","It doubles."],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it the same flow of charge?","explain":"The same charge flows each second, so the current is the same size."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"When was the electron discovered?","choices":["1897","1747","1820"],"answer":0,"hint":"It was the last of these events.","explain":"Thomson found it in 1897. Franklin named + and − in 1747; Ampère set the current direction in the 1820s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"About 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons carry 1 coulomb. Each second, {n} × 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons pass a point. What is the current in amps?","answer":"n","hint":"Count the coulombs passing each second: that is the current in amps.","explain":"{n} × 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons is {n} C each second, so the current is {n} A.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The cell in a loop is turned round. What happens to the arrows?","choices":["Both the current and electron arrows reverse.","Only the current arrows reverse.","Nothing changes."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which terminal is now on each side?","explain":"+ and − swap sides, so the current reverses, and the electrons, always opposite, reverse too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which statement about conventional current is true?","choices":["It is the direction positive charge would move.","It is the direction electrons move in a wire.","It was chosen after the electron was found."],"answer":0,"hint":"Conventional current is defined by positive charge.","explain":"It is the way positive charge would move. In metals the electrons go the other way, and the rule came first."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A current of {i} A flows for {t} s. How much charge passes, in coulombs? (Q = I × t)","answer":"i*t","hint":"Multiply the current by the time.","explain":"Q = {i} × {t} = {=i*t} C, whichever direction picture you use.","params":{"i":[1,5],"t":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"In salt water, which charges carry the current?","choices":["Positive and negative ions, moving opposite ways","Only electrons","Only positive ions"],"answer":0,"hint":"Salt water has no free electrons; it has ions.","explain":"Positive ions move with conventional current and negative ions against it, so neither picture is the whole story there."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A current arrow I points to the right along a wire. Which way do the electrons drift?","choices":["left","right","they do not move"],"answer":0,"hint":"Electrons are negative. Conventional current is the way positive charge would go.","explain":"Electrons drift opposite to conventional current, so they go left."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A charge of {=i*t} C passes a point in a wire in {t} s. What is the current in amps? (I = Q ÷ t)","answer":"i","hint":"Divide the charge by the time. The direction you picture does not change the size.","explain":"I = {=i*t} ÷ {t} = {i} A, whether you picture + charge one way or electrons the other way.","params":{"i":[1,6],"t":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why is conventional current drawn from + to −?","choices":["The direction was chosen before the electron was discovered.","Electrons flow from + to −.","Positive charges always move in metal wires."],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the dates: the 1740s and 1820s, and 1897.","explain":"Franklin's and Ampère's choices came long before Thomson found the negative electron in 1897."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An electron drifts about 0.1 mm along a wire each second. How many seconds does it take to drift {d} mm?","answer":"d*10","hint":"Each second it moves 0.1 mm, so 1 mm takes 10 seconds.","explain":"{d} mm × 10 s per mm = {=d*10} s. Electrons drift slowly, even though the lamp lights almost at once.","params":{"d":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"An LED's triangle points left. Conventional current reaches it flowing right. What happens?","choices":["No current flows, so it stays off.","It lights, because electrons go left.","The current turns round."],"answer":0,"hint":"Diode symbols are drawn for conventional current.","explain":"The current is against the triangle, so the diode blocks it and the LED stays off."}],"explain":{"kid":["On a circuit diagram, the current arrow starts at the + end of the cell. That is the long line. It goes round the loop and back to the − end, the short line. We call this conventional current.","In a metal wire, the things that move are electrons. Electrons are negative. The − end pushes them away and the + end pulls them in. So they drift from − to +. That is the other way to the arrows.","Both pictures give the same current. A negative charge moving left does the same job as a positive charge moving right. So the ammeter reads the same, and the sums do not change.","The two ways come from history. In the 1740s, Benjamin Franklin named charges + and −. He guessed that + charge moves. In the 1820s, André-Marie Ampère set current to flow from + to −. The electron was found in 1897 by J. J. Thomson. It was negative. By then, all the rules and symbols used the old way, and they still work."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. GCSE Physics (for example AQA 8463, section 4.2.1) defines current as the rate of flow of charge, Q = It. The diode symbol points the way conventional current can pass. OpenStax College Physics 2e (section 20.1) defines conventional current as the way positive charge would flow. It notes that in metals the moving charges are electrons, which go the other way.","The history: Franklin's letters of 1747 brought in the labels positive and negative. Volta's pile of 1800 gave the first steady current. Ampère, in the 1820s, set the direction of current as the way positive charge moves. Thomson found the electron, and its negative charge, in 1897. In other conductors the carriers differ. In salt water, both positive and negative ions move. In p-type semiconductors, the carriers act like positive holes. So the convention is not simply wrong.","Common mistakes: thinking electrons leave the + terminal; thinking the two pictures give different currents or opposite meter readings; thinking electrons race round at near light speed (their drift is often well under 1 mm per second, while the push along the wire sets up almost at once); reading a diode symbol by electron flow; and thinking the + to − rule was chosen after the electron was found.","In the lab you drag along each wire to mark the current, then turn the cell round and do it again. You mark the electron drift against the current arrows and watch electrons move. You judge whether an LED lights in four circuits, put four events in order, and answer four questions that show both pictures give the same current."]},"familyTip":"Draw a simple loop with a cell on the back of an envelope and ask your teen to add a current arrow and an electron arrow. Then turn the cell round and ask what changes. Ask why an LED only lights one way round, and which arrow its symbol follows.","keyIdea":"Conventional current is drawn from the positive terminal of a cell, round the circuit, to the negative terminal. In a metal wire the charges that actually move are electrons, which are negative, so they drift the opposite way: out of the negative terminal and round to the positive. Both descriptions give the same current, because negative charge moving one way has the same effect as positive charge moving the other way. The convention stays because it was set in the 1700s and 1800s, long before the electron was discovered in 1897, and circuit rules and symbols such as the diode arrow were built on it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which statements describe conventional current, and which describe electron flow? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["conventional current","electron flow"],"items":[["leaves the + terminal",0],["leaves the − terminal",1],["what diode arrows follow",0],["negative charges drifting",1],["set before 1897",0],["drift under 1 mm/s",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcurdir","caption":"Flagship lab: drag current and electron arrows round a circuit, flip the cell, judge if an LED lights, order the history, and test that both give one current.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_GHtAwst9_J":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which graph shape shows an LDR's resistance against light intensity?","choices":["Falls steeply, then levels off","A straight line going up","Flat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about dark (very high) and bright (low).","explain":"Resistance drops a lot as it first gets lighter, then changes little in bright light."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How is a thermistor different from a metal wire when both get hotter?","choices":["The thermistor's resistance falls; the wire's rises","Both rise","Both fall"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture both graphs as the temperature goes up: does each line climb or fall?","explain":"A (negative temperature coefficient) thermistor loses resistance when hot. A metal wire gains resistance."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"On a 10 V supply, a thermistor of {t} kΩ is in series with a fixed resistor of {=10-t} kΩ. What is the pd across the fixed resistor, in volts?","answer":"10-t","hint":"The total is 10 kΩ, so each kΩ gets 1 V of the 10 V.","explain":"The fixed resistor has {=10-t} of the 10 kΩ, so it gets {=10-t} V. The thermistor gets {t} V.","params":{"t":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A night light reads the pd across the LDR. It turns on at 3 V. Why does it come on in the dark?","choices":["The LDR's resistance rises, so its share of the pd rises","The LDR makes its own voltage in the dark","The battery voltage goes up at night"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the LDR's resistance in the dark?","explain":"In the dark the LDR has more resistance, so it takes a bigger share of the supply pd and passes 3 V."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A fire alarm reads the pd across a fixed resistor in series with a thermistor. It rings at room temperature. What should you change?","choices":["Use a smaller fixed resistor","Use a bigger fixed resistor","Use a bigger battery"],"answer":0,"hint":"With a smaller fixed resistor, does the thermistor need to fall further before 3 V?","explain":"A smaller fixed resistor needs the thermistor's resistance to fall further, so the alarm waits for a higher temperature."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A thermistor has {n} times the resistance of the fixed resistor in series with it. The supply is {=3*(n+1)} V. What is the pd across the thermistor, in volts?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Split the supply into {=n+1} equal shares: {n} for the thermistor, 1 for the fixed resistor.","explain":"{=3*(n+1)} V ÷ {=n+1} shares = 3 V per share. The thermistor has {n} shares: {=3*n} V.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which pair is matched correctly?","choices":["Automatic street light: LDR","Oven thermostat: LDR","Burglar alarm light beam: thermistor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Match light to LDR and temperature to thermistor.","explain":"A street light senses dusk, so it uses an LDR. An oven senses temperature (thermistor) and a light beam senses light (LDR)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a sensing circuit, why is the sensor put in series with a fixed resistor?","choices":["So the changing resistance becomes a changing pd","So the sensor does not get hot","So the current stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does sharing the supply pd do?","explain":"As the sensor's resistance changes, its share of the supply pd changes, giving a voltage that a switch can read."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An LDR moves from a dark cupboard into sunlight. What happens to its resistance?","choices":["It falls","It rises","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"LDR stands for light-dependent resistor. Which way does light push it?","explain":"More light, less resistance. It might drop from about 30 kΩ to a few hundred ohms."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A thermistor (in series with a fixed resistor) has {r} kΩ and the fixed resistor has {r} kΩ, on a 6 V supply. What is the pd across the thermistor, in volts?","answer":"3","hint":"Equal resistances in series share the pd equally.","explain":"The resistances are equal, so each part gets half of 6 V: 3 V, whatever {r} kΩ is.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which device most likely uses a thermistor?","choices":["A kettle that switches off at boiling","A street lamp that turns on at dusk","A phone screen that dims in the dark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is sensing temperature, not light?","explain":"A kettle senses temperature, so it uses a thermistor. The other two sense light, so they use LDRs."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"On a 12 V supply, an LDR of {a} kΩ is in series with a fixed resistor of {=12-a} kΩ, so the total is 12 kΩ. What is the pd across the LDR, in volts?","answer":"a","hint":"Each part gets the same fraction of the 12 V as its fraction of the 12 kΩ total.","explain":"The LDR has {a} of the 12 kΩ, so it gets {a} of the 12 V: {a} V. The fixed resistor gets {=12-a} V.","params":{"a":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A thermistor is in series with a fixed resistor. It gets hotter. What happens to the pd across the fixed resistor?","choices":["It rises","It falls","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The thermistor's resistance falls. Which part now has a bigger share?","explain":"The thermistor loses resistance, so the fixed resistor has a bigger share of the total and its pd rises."}],"explain":{"kid":["Most resistors keep the same resistance. An LDR and a thermistor do not. An LDR is a light-dependent resistor. Shine more light on it and its resistance goes down. In the dark it can be about 30 kΩ. In daylight it can drop to a few hundred ohms.","A thermistor changes with temperature. Heat it and its resistance goes down. A typical one is 10 kΩ at 25 °C and under 1 kΩ in boiling water. A metal wire does the opposite: it gets more resistance when it gets hot. For both sensors the graph drops steeply at first, then levels off.","To use a sensor, put it in series with a fixed resistor across a battery. The voltage is shared, and the part with more resistance gets a bigger share. When an LDR goes dark, its resistance rises, so it takes more of the voltage. A switch that reads the voltage across the LDR can turn a lamp on at night.","A fire alarm works the other way round. As the thermistor heats up, its resistance falls, so the fixed resistor gets more of the voltage. Pick the fixed resistor so its share reaches the switch-on voltage at the right temperature. A smaller fixed resistor makes the alarm wait until it is hotter."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. AQA GCSE Physics 4.2.1.4 states that the resistance of a thermistor decreases as the temperature increases and the resistance of an LDR decreases as light intensity increases. Pupils must know uses such as thermostats and lights that switch on when it gets dark, and sketch both resistance graphs. OCR Gateway and Edexcel have matching statements. Sharing pd in series (AQA 4.2.2) is what makes a sensor useful. The pd across each part is in the same ratio as the resistances.","The lab uses model parts with typical datasheet values. The LDR follows R = 20 kΩ × (10 lux ÷ E)^0.7, which gives about 32 kΩ at 5 lux and 260 Ω at 5000 lux. The thermistor is a 10 kΩ NTC type with B = 3950 K, about 34 kΩ at 0 °C and 0.7 kΩ at 100 °C. Pupils plot both graphs. They then decide which part a switch should read for a night light on 6 V with a 10 kΩ resistor. Last, they find the fixed resistor (3.6 kΩ) that makes a fire alarm reach 3 V at 50 °C, and sort six real devices.","Common mistakes. Pupils often reverse the LDR, thinking more light means more resistance. Many assume a thermistor behaves like a metal wire, whose resistance rises with temperature. In sensing circuits they read the wrong part of the divider. Some think the sensor takes all the supply pd, or that the voltage across the LDR falls in the dark. When tuning an alarm, many choose a bigger fixed resistor to make it ring later, which does the opposite."]},"familyTip":"Look for an automatic night light or a street lamp that turns on at dusk; both use an LDR. Ask your teenager to explain why covering the sensor with a thumb in daylight turns the light on, and what is inside the house that needs a thermistor (a boiler, fridge, oven or kettle).","keyIdea":"An LDR (light-dependent resistor) and a thermistor are sensors whose resistance changes with their surroundings. The resistance of an LDR decreases as light intensity increases: a typical LDR is about 30 kΩ in the dark and a few hundred ohms in daylight. The resistance of a thermistor decreases as temperature increases: a typical one is 10 kΩ at 25 °C and under 1 kΩ at 100 °C, the opposite of a metal wire. Both graphs fall steeply at first and then level off. In a sensing circuit the sensor sits in series with a fixed resistor across a supply. The total pd is shared in proportion to resistance, so as the sensor's resistance changes, the pd across each part changes. LDRs switch street lights on at dusk; thermistors run thermostats and fire alarms.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What happens to the resistance? Drag each change to a box, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Resistance goes down","Resistance goes up"],"items":[["An LDR in brighter light",0],["A thermistor is heated",0],["An LDR is covered up",1],["A thermistor is put in iced water",1],["A metal wire gets hotter",1],["An LDR goes from shade into sunshine",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsensor","caption":"Flagship lab: plot an LDR against light and a thermistor against temperature, wire a night light, set a 50 °C fire alarm, sort sensor devices.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
